<!-- CHAPTER 1 -->
<h1>Chapter 1: The Project in Simple Words (No Jargon)</h1>

<p>
Imagine you are building a complete online store like Amazon or Flipkart, but built entirely from scratch using pure Java. That is <strong>ShopSphere</strong>.
</p>

<h3>1.1 What is ShopSphere?</h3>
<p>
ShopSphere is an <strong>Enterprise E-Commerce Management System</strong>. It allows everyday customers to browse products on a website, add them to a shopping cart, apply discount coupon codes, and place orders. At the same time, it gives the business owner (the Administrator) tools to manage inventory, change order statuses, add new products, and track warehouse stock.
</p>

<h3>1.2 The Three Different Interfaces (Clients) Built into ShopSphere</h3>
<p>
Most student projects only have a basic website. ShopSphere is unique and impressive because it includes <strong>three separate applications</strong> that all talk to the same database:
</p>
<ol>
    <li>
        <strong>The Web Storefront (Website):</strong>
        Opened in any web browser (Google Chrome, Edge, Safari). Used by customers to view laptops, phones, headphones, add items to cart, and checkout. Also used by admins to manage the store online.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The Desktop Admin Application (Java Swing):</strong>
        A real desktop window with buttons, menus, and tables (like an MS Excel or Tally app) that opens on your computer. Used by warehouse managers to quickly restock products and update order statuses with 1 click without needing a web browser.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The Distributed Network Systems (TCP Sockets & RMI):</strong>
        Two background server systems. One acts like a walkie-talkie for barcode scanners in a warehouse (TCP Sockets on port 5055). The other allows other computers to execute inventory methods remotely (Java RMI on port 1099).
    </li>
</ol>

<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>💡 The Restaurant Analogy:</strong><br>
    Think of ShopSphere like a modern restaurant:
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The Customers</strong> sit in the dining hall and read the printed menu (The Website / JSP).</li>
        <li><strong>The Waiters</strong> take customer orders and carry them to the kitchen (The Servlets / Controllers).</li>
        <li><strong>The Kitchen Chef</strong> checks recipes, cooks food, and updates ingredients (Business Services).</li>
        <li><strong>The Stockroom / Pantry</strong> stores all food ingredients in organized shelves (The MySQL Database).</li>
        <li><strong>The Manager's Office Computer</strong> has a desktop application showing how many veggies are left (Java Swing Desktop App).</li>
        <li><strong>The Delivery Truck Walkie-Talkie</strong> tells the kitchen when fresh stock arrives (TCP Sockets & RMI).</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 2 -->
<h1>Chapter 2: Core Concepts Explained with Real-Life Analogies</h1>

<p>
If the examiner asks you "What is a Servlet?" or "What is JDBC?", do not panic! Here are the core concepts explained in everyday language:
</p>

<h3>2.1 What is a Database (MySQL)?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> Think of MySQL like a collection of organized <strong>Microsoft Excel spreadsheets</strong> stored on your computer.
</div>
<p>
Instead of saving user accounts and products in text files (which would get messy and slow), we use <strong>MySQL</strong>. Each "sheet" in our database is called a <strong>Table</strong>. ShopSphere has 12 tables:
<code>users</code>, <code>products</code>, <code>categories</code>, <code>cart</code>, <code>wishlist</code>, <code>orders</code>, <code>order_items</code>, <code>payments</code>, <code>coupons</code>, <code>addresses</code>, <code>reviews</code>, and <code>coupon_usage</code>.
</p>

<h3>2.2 What is JDBC (Java Database Connectivity)?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> MySQL speaks SQL language (like French), and Java speaks Java (like English). <strong>JDBC is the translator and the telephone cable</strong> connecting them.
</div>
<p>
JDBC allows Java code to send SQL commands (like <code>SELECT * FROM products</code>) to MySQL and receive the rows back as Java objects. In ShopSphere, we use <strong>Plain JDBC</strong> with <code>PreparedStatement</code> (we do NOT use frameworks like Hibernate or Spring Boot, because RTU syllabus tests your core Java knowledge).
</p>

<h3>2.3 What is a Servlet?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> A Servlet is the <strong>waiter in a restaurant</strong>.
</div>
<p>
When a user clicks "Login" on their browser, the browser sends an HTTP request over the internet. The <strong>Servlet</strong> catches that request, reads what email and password the user typed, asks the Java service to verify it against the database, and then decides what web page the user should see next.
</p>

<h3>2.4 What is a JSP (JavaServer Pages)?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> JSP is the <strong>printed menu card</strong> with blanks that get filled in dynamically.
</div>
<p>
HTML is static—it cannot change by itself. <strong>JSP</strong> is HTML mixed with special tags. When a customer opens the product page, the JSP dynamically prints the product's actual name, picture, and price taken from the database.
</p>

<h3>2.5 What is a Session and a Cookie?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A Cookie</strong> is like a <strong>hand-stamp</strong> you get at an amusement park. It is stored on your own hand (your browser).</li>
        <li><strong>An HttpSession</strong> is like a <strong>locker inside the park</strong>. The park keeps your bags safely in the locker (server memory), and gives you a locker number (Session ID) stamped on your ticket.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
<p>
HTTP is "stateless"—it forgets who you are the second a page loads. <strong>Sessions</strong> allow the server to remember: <em>"Aha! This user logged in 5 minutes ago as admin@shopsphere.com, so keep them logged in while they click around!"</em>
</p>

<h3>2.6 What is a Filter?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> A Filter is a <strong>security guard standing at the front door</strong> of the building.
</div>
<p>
Before any request is allowed to reach a Servlet, it must pass through our Filters:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>EncodingFilter</code>: Ensures all characters are UTF-8 (so Hindi and special symbols work).</li>
    <li><code>AuthenticationFilter</code>: Checks: <em>"Are you logged in? If not, stop right there! Go to the login page first."</em></li>
    <li><code>AdminFilter</code>: Checks: <em>"Are you an Admin? If you are just a regular customer, you are not allowed in the admin area!"</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>2.7 What is Java Swing?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> Swing is Java's tool to draw <strong>real Windows desktop applications</strong> (windows, buttons, checkboxes, tables).
</div>
<p>
Instead of a website in a browser, <code>shopsphere-swing-admin</code> is a standalone program that opens its own desktop window with menus (File, Look & Feel, Help) and tabs to manage products, categories, customers, and orders.
</p>

<h3>2.8 What is a TCP Socket?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> A Socket is like a <strong>direct phone call or walkie-talkie</strong> between two computers.
</div>
<p>
In <code>shopsphere-network</code>, computer A connects directly to computer B on port 5055. Computer A sends a message: <code>CHECK_STOCK:1</code>, and Computer B immediately replies: <code>SUCCESS:STOCK:25</code>.
</p>

<h3>2.9 What is Java RMI (Remote Method Invocation)?</h3>
<div class="analogy-box">
    <strong>Analogy:</strong> RMI is like a <strong>magical remote control</strong> that presses a button on a machine in another room.
</div>
<p>
Normally, in Java, you can only call methods on objects in your own program (like <code>service.getStock(1)</code>). With <strong>RMI</strong>, your Java program can call a method that actually runs inside a completely different Java program running on another machine across the network, and get the result back transparently!
</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 3 -->
<h1>Chapter 3: What Was Implemented (Feature-by-Feature Tour)</h1>

<p>
Here is the exact list of all features implemented in ShopSphere. You can speak about any of these in your viva:
</p>

<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Feature Name</th>
        <th style="width: 35%;">What It Does in the Application</th>
        <th style="width: 40%;">How We Implemented It in Code</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>User Registration & Login</strong></td>
        <td>Allows customers to sign up with name, email, phone, and password. Allows admins and customers to log in securely.</td>
        <td>
            <code>LoginServlet.java</code> & <code>RegisterServlet.java</code>.<br>
            Passwords are encrypted using <strong>PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256</strong> (salted hash with 65,536 iterations). We never store raw passwords.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Session Fixation Defense</strong></td>
        <td>Prevents hackers from stealing a user's login session.</td>
        <td>
            In <code>LoginServlet</code>, right after credentials match, we call <code>request.changeSessionId()</code>. This destroys the old guest session ID and issues a brand-new secure ID.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Product Catalog & Search</strong></td>
        <td>Displays items with images, prices, stock badges, rating stars, keyword search bar, and category filters.</td>
        <td>
            <code>ProductServlet.java</code> handles <code>/products</code>.<br>
            Executes SQL <code>SELECT ... WHERE category_id=? AND price BETWEEN ? AND ?</code> via <code>PreparedStatement</code>.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Shopping Cart & Wishlist</strong></td>
        <td>Customers can add items to cart, change quantities (+/-), save items to wishlist, and view live totals.</td>
        <td>
            <code>CartServlet.java</code> and <code>WishlistServlet.java</code>.<br>
            All totals (Subtotal, GST 18%, Shipping) are computed <strong>server-side</strong> in Java so clients cannot tamper with prices.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Discount Coupons Engine</strong></td>
        <td>Customers enter codes like <code>WELCOME10</code> or <code>FESTIVE20</code> to get percentage or flat discounts.</td>
        <td>
            <code>CouponService.java</code> validates expiry date, minimum order value, and maximum usage count before subtracting discount.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Atomic Checkout (ACID)</strong></td>
        <td>Places the order, creates order line items, deducts stock, records payment, and empties the cart.</td>
        <td>
            <code>CheckoutService.java</code>.<br>
            Uses <code>conn.setAutoCommit(false)</code>, locks stock with <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>, and calls <code>conn.commit()</code>. If anything fails, it calls <code>conn.rollback()</code>!
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Web Admin Dashboard</strong></td>
        <td>Admins view revenue charts, total orders count, product CRUD, and update order statuses (PENDING &rarr; SHIPPED).</td>
        <td>
            <code>AdminDashboardServlet.java</code> and admin JSPs.<br>
            Guarded by <code>AdminFilter</code> so standard customers get HTTP 403 Access Denied.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Java Swing Desktop App</strong></td>
        <td>A standalone desktop window for warehouse managers with tables, forms, and look-and-feel switcher.</td>
        <td>
            <code>SwingAdminApp.java</code> in <code>swing-admin</code> module.<br>
            Follows MVC pattern. Uses <code>SwingWorker</code> to run database queries on background threads so the GUI never freezes.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>TCP Socket Inventory Server</strong></td>
        <td>Listens on port 5055 for barcode terminals to check or update stock via network commands.</td>
        <td>
            <code>InventoryServer.java</code> uses <code>java.net.ServerSocket</code> and a <code>FixedThreadPool(10)</code> to handle multiple client connections concurrently.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Java Remote Method Invocation</strong></td>
        <td>Distributed service allowing remote JVMs to check stock and inventory over port 1099.</td>
        <td>
            <code>InventoryService.java</code> (Remote interface), <code>InventoryServiceImpl.java</code> (extends <code>UnicastRemoteObject</code>), and <code>RmiServer.java</code> (RMI Registry).
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Internationalization (i18n)</strong></td>
        <td>Switch entire website language between English and Hindi (हिंदी) with Indian Rupee (₹) formatting.</td>
        <td>
            <code>messages_en.properties</code> & <code>messages_hi.properties</code>.<br>
            <code>LanguageServlet.java</code> switches locale via <code>fmt:setLocale</code> and renders text via <code>fmt:message</code>.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Syllabus Academic Demo Hub</strong></td>
        <td>A live page demonstrating all university syllabus topics (EL, JSTL XML, JSTL SQL, JNDI, JSP elements).</td>
        <td>
            <code>/demo/index.jsp</code> linking 7 interactive demonstration pages.
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

