<!-- CHAPTER 4 -->
<h1>Chapter 4: How It Works Under the Hood (Step-by-Step Code Traces)</h1>

<p>
Examiners love asking: <em>"Walk me through what happens when a user clicks a button."</em> Here are the two most important flows explained step-by-step:
</p>

<h3>4.1 Trace 1: What happens when a user logs in?</h3>
<ol>
    <li><strong>User action:</strong> The user types <code>admin@shopsphere.com</code> and password <code>Admin@123</code> into <code>login.jsp</code> and clicks <strong>"Sign In"</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Browser sends HTTP POST:</strong> The browser sends an HTTP POST request to the URL <code>/shopsphere/login</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Filters intercept:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><code>EncodingFilter</code> intercepts the request and sets character encoding to <code>UTF-8</code>.</li>
            <li><code>SecurityFilter</code> injects browser security headers (XSS and Clickjacking protection).</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Servlet receives request:</strong> Tomcat routes the request to <code>LoginServlet.doPost()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Service call:</strong> <code>LoginServlet</code> calls <code>UserService.authenticate(email, password)</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Database lookup:</strong> <code>UserDAOImpl</code> executes a safe parameterized query:
        <pre>SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?</pre>
        It gets the user record containing the stored salt and password hash.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Password verification:</strong> <code>PasswordUtil</code> hashes the entered password with the user's stored salt using <strong>PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256</strong>. If the generated hash matches the stored hash, authentication succeeds!</li>
    <li><strong>Session Fixation Defense:</strong> <code>LoginServlet</code> calls <code>request.changeSessionId()</code> to rotate the session ID safely.</li>
    <li><strong>Store in Session:</strong> The servlet saves the user object in the session:
        <pre>session.setAttribute("currentUser", user);</pre>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Redirect:</strong> The servlet sends an HTTP 302 Redirect to <code>/shopsphere/home</code> (or <code>/admin/dashboard</code>). The browser displays the logged-in homepage!</li>
</ol>

<hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin: 15pt 0;">

<h3>4.2 Trace 2: What happens when a customer places an order (Atomic Checkout)?</h3>
<div class="warning-box">
    <strong>⭐ Must Know For Viva:</strong> This demonstrates <strong>ACID Transactions</strong>, which is a favorite topic of university examiners!
</div>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Customer clicks "Place Order"</strong> on <code>checkout.jsp</code>.</li>
    <li><code>CheckoutServlet.doPost()</code> catches the request and calls <code>CheckoutService.processCheckout()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Start Transaction:</strong> <code>CheckoutService</code> gets a database connection and turns off auto-commit:
        <pre>conn.setAutoCommit(false); // Begin ACID Transaction</pre>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Step A (Lock & Check Stock):</strong> For every item in the cart, it runs:
        <pre>SELECT stock_quantity FROM products WHERE id = ? FOR UPDATE;</pre>
        If the customer wants 2 laptops, but only 1 is left in stock, it immediately throws an error and cancels everything.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Step B (Deduct Stock):</strong> It subtracts the purchased quantity from <code>products.stock_quantity</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Step C (Calculate Math Server-Side):</strong> It calculates Subtotal, applies coupon discount (if any), calculates 18% GST, adds shipping fee, and gets the Net Total.</li>
    <li><strong>Step D (Insert Order Header):</strong> It inserts a new row into the <code>orders</code> table and gets the generated Order ID.</li>
    <li><strong>Step E (Insert Order Items):</strong> For each cart item, it inserts a row into <code>order_items</code> (freezing the historical purchase price).</li>
    <li><strong>Step F (Insert Payment & Clear Cart):</strong> It records the payment row and deletes the user's items from the <code>cart</code> table.</li>
    <li><strong>Commit Transaction:</strong>
        <pre>conn.commit(); // Everything succeeded! Save permanently.</pre>
        <em>If any error occurred at ANY step above, the <code>catch</code> block executes:</em>
        <pre>conn.rollback(); // Undo EVERYTHING. Database stays clean and safe!</pre>
    </li>
</ol>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 5 -->
<h1>Chapter 5: Technical Terms & Buzzwords Glossary</h1>

<p>
Memorize these short, crisp definitions. When the examiner uses these words, you will know exactly what they mean:
</p>

<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Term</th>
        <th style="width: 45%;">Simple Definition</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Where in ShopSphere?</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>PreparedStatement</strong></td>
        <td>A pre-compiled SQL query using <code>?</code> placeholders. Prevents SQL injection attacks and executes faster.</td>
        <td>Used in all 11 DAOs (<code>ProductDAOImpl</code>, <code>UserDAOImpl</code>, etc.).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>SQL Injection (SQLi)</strong></td>
        <td>A cyberattack where a hacker types SQL commands into input boxes (like <code>' OR 1=1 --</code>) to steal data.</td>
        <td>We defeated SQLi 100% by using <code>PreparedStatement</code> everywhere.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</strong></td>
        <td>An attack where malicious JavaScript is injected into web pages.</td>
        <td>Defeated by escaping all text using JSTL <code>&lt;c:out&gt;</code> and SecurityFilter headers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>N-Tier Architecture</strong></td>
        <td>Separating an application into distinct layers (Presentation &rarr; Controller &rarr; Service &rarr; DAO &rarr; Database) so each layer only does its own job.</td>
        <td>Our whole project structure (Web/Swing &rarr; Servlets &rarr; Services &rarr; DAOs &rarr; MySQL).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>MVC (Model-View-Controller)</strong></td>
        <td>
            <strong>Model:</strong> Data (Product, User).<br>
            <strong>View:</strong> Screen (JSP, Swing Panel).<br>
            <strong>Controller:</strong> Logic coordinator (Servlet, Action Listener).
        </td>
        <td>Implemented in both our Web application and our Swing Desktop Admin app.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Event Dispatch Thread (EDT)</strong></td>
        <td>A single special background thread in Java Swing responsible for drawing windows and handling mouse clicks.</td>
        <td>In <code>SwingAdminApp.java</code>, all GUI code is launched via <code>SwingUtilities.invokeLater()</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>SwingWorker</strong></td>
        <td>A background worker thread in Swing. Used to run slow tasks (like database queries) off the EDT so the desktop window doesn't freeze or lag.</td>
        <td>Used in <code>ProductController</code>, <code>OrderController</code> in the Swing admin module.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Pluggable Look & Feel (PLAF)</strong></td>
        <td>The ability of a Java Swing app to switch its visual theme (System, Nimbus, Metal) at runtime without restarting.</td>
        <td>In <code>AdminDashboard.java</code> under the "Look & Feel" menu bar.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>RMI Registry</strong></td>
        <td>A naming directory service running on port 1099 that maps service names (like <code>"InventoryService"</code>) to remote objects.</td>
        <td>Created by <code>RmiServer.java</code> using <code>LocateRegistry.createRegistry(1099)</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Stub & Skeleton</strong></td>
        <td>
            <strong>Stub:</strong> Client-side proxy object.<br>
            <strong>Skeleton:</strong> Server-side dispatcher object that receives the remote call and runs it.
        </td>
        <td>Core mechanism of our <code>shopsphere-rmi</code> module.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Serialization</strong></td>
        <td>Converting an in-memory Java object into a sequence of bytes so it can be saved to disk or sent over a network.</td>
        <td>Domain classes implement <code>java.io.Serializable</code>. Demonstrated in <code>SerializationDemo.java</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>transient keyword</strong></td>
        <td>Tells Java: <em>"Do NOT serialize this field when saving/transmitting this object!"</em> Used for sensitive data like passwords.</td>
        <td>Used in <code>SerializationDemo.java</code> to hide user passwords during network transmission.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>JNDI (Java Naming & Directory Interface)</strong></td>
        <td>A standard API for looking up resources by name (like looking up a phone number in a phone book).</td>
        <td>Used in <code>demo/jndi-demo.jsp</code> to look up Tomcat's database pool <code>jdbc/shopsphereDS</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>i18n (Internationalization)</strong></td>
        <td>Designing software so it can be adapted to various languages and regions without code changes.</td>
        <td>Demonstrated by our English / Hindi switcher using <code>ResourceBundle</code> and JSTL <code>fmt:message</code>.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 6 -->
<h1>Chapter 6: Top 25 Viva Questions & Exactly What to Say</h1>

<p>
Read these 25 questions and practice speaking the answers out loud. These cover 95% of questions external examiners ask:
</p>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q1: What is the difference between <code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code> HTTP methods?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"Sir, GET is used to retrieve data from the server. The parameters are visible in the URL bar, and it has a length limit. POST is used to submit or save sensitive data (like passwords or payments). The data is sent inside the HTTP request body, so it is not visible in the URL bar and has no size limit."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q2: What is the lifecycle of a Servlet?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"A Servlet has three main lifecycle stages managed by the web container (Tomcat):<br>
    1. <strong>init():</strong> Called once when the servlet is first created to initialize resources.<br>
    2. <strong>service():</strong> Called on every request. It dispatches to doGet() or doPost() to handle the client.<br>
    3. <strong>destroy():</strong> Called once when the server shuts down to clean up connections."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q3: What is the difference between <code>forward()</code> and <code>sendRedirect()</code>?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"RequestDispatcher.forward() is a <strong>server-side</strong> transfer. The URL in the browser does NOT change, request attributes are preserved, and only 1 network round-trip occurs. On the other hand, response.sendRedirect() is a <strong>client-side</strong> redirect. The server sends HTTP 302 to the browser, the browser makes a completely new request, the URL changes, and old request attributes are lost."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q4: What is the difference between a Statement and a PreparedStatement?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"Statement compiles the SQL query every single time it runs and concatenates raw text, which makes it vulnerable to SQL Injection attacks. PreparedStatement is pre-compiled by the database engine and uses question mark (?) placeholders. It is faster for repeated executions and provides 100% protection against SQL Injection."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q5: What is an ACID transaction? How is it implemented in your project?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability. In ShopSphere, we implement this in CheckoutService during checkout. We call <code>conn.setAutoCommit(false)</code> to begin the transaction. We check and lock stock with row-level locks, insert the order, insert items, record payment, and clear the cart. If all succeed, we call <code>conn.commit()</code>. If any step fails, we catch the exception and call <code>conn.rollback()</code> so no half-completed orders exist."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q6: Why did you not use scriptlets <code>&lt;% %&gt;</code> in your business JSPs?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"Sir, writing raw Java scriptlets inside JSPs violates the MVC architecture by mixing business logic with presentation markup. It makes code difficult to maintain, read, and test. In ShopSphere, all business logic is in Servlets and Services, and our JSPs use clean Expression Language (${...}) and JSTL tags."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q7: What are the 4 scopes of Expression Language (EL)?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"EL searches for attributes across four scopes in order from narrowest to widest: 1. pageScope, 2. requestScope, 3. sessionScope, and 4. applicationScope. If not found in any scope, it returns null without throwing a NullPointerException."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q8: What is a Servlet Filter? Where is it used in ShopSphere?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"A Filter is an interceptor that executes before requests reach a servlet or after responses leave it. We implemented a 4-tier filter chain: EncodingFilter for UTF-8 encoding, SecurityFilter for browser defense headers, AuthenticationFilter to guard customer pages like /cart and /checkout, and AdminFilter to enforce role-based access control on /admin routes."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q9: What is Session Fixation, and how did you prevent it?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"Session Fixation is an attack where an attacker tricks a victim into authenticating using a pre-existing session ID known to the attacker. In LoginServlet, immediately after verifying valid credentials, we call <code>request.changeSessionId()</code>. This generates a brand-new cryptographically secure session ID, completely neutralizing the attack."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q10: How does your password hashing work?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"We do not store passwords in plain text or simple MD5. We use salted PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256. It generates a 128-bit random salt and runs 65,536 iterations. The salt and hash are stored together in the database, making rainbow-table and dictionary attacks practically impossible."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q11: What is the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT) in Java Swing?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"Swing components are not thread-safe. All UI creation, painting, and user event handling must run on a single dedicated thread called the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT). If we run long database calls on the EDT, the window freezes. So we use <code>SwingWorker</code> to run DB operations on a background thread and update the UI when finished."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q12: What is the difference between TCP Sockets and Java RMI?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"TCP Sockets operate at the transport layer (byte stream). We have to design our own custom text protocol (like CHECK_STOCK:1) and manually parse strings. Java RMI operates at the application/object layer. It allows one JVM to invoke actual Java methods on an object in another JVM with typed parameters and return values, handling serialization automatically."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q13: What does the <code>transient</code> keyword do in Java?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"The transient keyword indicates that a variable should NOT be serialized when an object is converted into a byte stream. During deserialization, transient fields are reset to their default values (null or 0). In our project, we mark sensitive password fields transient to prevent them from leaking across network streams."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q14: What is JNDI and why is it used?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"Java Naming and Directory Interface is a directory lookup service. Instead of hardcoding database passwords inside Java code, Tomcat manages a connection pool in context.xml. Our code simply looks up the DataSource using <code>new InitialContext().lookup(\"java:comp/env/jdbc/shopsphereDS\")</code>. This decouples database configuration from application code."</em>
</div>

<div class="viva-box">
    <strong>Q15: How did you implement Internationalization (i18n)?</strong><br>
    <strong>What to say:</strong><br>
    <em>"We created resource bundle properties files (messages_en.properties and messages_hi.properties). When the user clicks the Hindi language switcher, LanguageServlet sets the session locale to 'hi_IN'. In JSP, we use JSTL <code>fmt:setLocale</code> and <code>fmt:message</code> tags to render Hindi labels and format prices in Indian Rupees (₹) dynamically."</em>
</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 7 -->
<h1>Chapter 7: The 10-Minute Live Viva Demo Script</h1>

<p>
When the external examiner sits down and says: <em>"Okay, show me your project,"</em> follow this exact script. It takes 8 to 10 minutes and shows 100% syllabus mastery:
</p>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 1: Open the Storefront Homepage</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Open browser &rarr; navigate to <code>http://localhost:8080/shopsphere/</code></p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Sir, this is the ShopSphere customer storefront running on Apache Tomcat 11 using Jakarta EE 10. The layout is fully responsive using Bootstrap 5. All products and categories shown here are fetched dynamically from our MySQL database using plain JDBC with zero ORM overhead."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 2: Demonstrate Search, Filter & Cookies</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Click on "Products" in the navbar or select "Laptops" category &rarr; drag price slider &rarr; click on a product.</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Here is our faceted search engine managed by ProductServlet. When we filter by category and price range, it executes parameterized queries using PreparedStatement. When I click on a product to view details, ProductServlet writes the product ID to a client cookie named 'recent_products'. When we go back to the homepage, you can see this 'Recently Viewed' section populated directly from that cookie."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 3: Demonstrate Authentication & Security</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Click "Sign In" &rarr; click the demo button <strong>"Fill Customer Credentials"</strong> &rarr; click Sign In.</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Here is our authentication system. We do not store raw passwords in the database; we use salted PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 with 65,536 iterations. Notice that immediately upon login, LoginServlet executes <code>request.changeSessionId()</code>, which prevents Session Fixation attacks by rotating the session ID."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 4: Demonstrate Cart & Atomic Checkout</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Add a product to cart &rarr; go to <code>/cart</code> &rarr; change quantity &rarr; proceed to checkout &rarr; enter coupon code <code>WELCOME10</code> &rarr; click "Place Order".</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"All cart totals are calculated strictly server-side to prevent price tampering. During checkout, CheckoutService initiates an ACID transaction with <code>conn.setAutoCommit(false)</code>. It locks the product stock row using <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>, deducts inventory, creates the order and item records, records payment, and empties the cart before calling <code>conn.commit()</code>. If inventory was insufficient, it would call <code>conn.rollback()</code>."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 5: Demonstrate the Web Admin Portal & Access Control</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Sign out &rarr; log in as <code>admin@shopsphere.com</code> / <code>Admin@123</code> &rarr; go to <code>/admin/dashboard</code>.</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Here is the Web Administration Portal. It provides analytics, revenue totals, product CRUD, and order management. If a regular customer tries to access any /admin URL, our AdminFilter intercepts the request and throws an HTTP 403 Access Denied error."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 6: Demonstrate the Academic Syllabus Demo Hub</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Navigate to <code>http://localhost:8080/shopsphere/demo/</code></p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Sir, to demonstrate all RTU Advanced Java curriculum topics, we built this Syllabus Demo Hub:
    <ul>
        <li><strong>JSP Elements:</strong> Shows declarations, scriptlets, expressions, 9 implicit objects, and lifecycle counter.</li>
        <li><strong>EL Demo:</strong> Proves 4-tier scope resolution.</li>
        <li><strong>JSTL XML:</strong> Parses XML feeds with XPath.</li>
        <li><strong>JSTL SQL:</strong> Demonstrates direct database queries via tags.</li>
        <li><strong>JNDI:</strong> Looks up Tomcat's managed connection pool.</li>
        <li><strong>Session Inspector:</strong> Shows live session ID, timestamps, and client cookies.</li>
    </ul>"</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 7: Demonstrate Internationalization (i18n Hindi)</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Click "Language" in the navbar &rarr; select <strong>"हिंदी (Hindi)"</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Here is our internationalization feature. Notice that all navigation labels, buttons, and headers immediately switch to Hindi backed by messages_hi.properties, fmt:setLocale, and fmt:message tags, while keeping Indian Rupee (₹) currency formatting intact."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 8: Demonstrate the Java Swing Desktop Admin App</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> In command prompt, run: <code>java -jar swing-admin/target/shopsphere-swing-admin-1.0.0-jar-with-dependencies.jar</code> &rarr; log in &rarr; switch Look & Feel to "Nimbus".</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"This is our Java Swing Desktop Administration application. It follows strict MVC architecture with custom AbstractTableModels. All database calls execute on background threads via SwingWorker to keep the Event Dispatch Thread completely responsive. We also support runtime Pluggable Look and Feel (PLAF)—notice how the theme instantly changes between System, Nimbus, and Metal."</em></p>
</div>

<div class="script-box">
    <h4>Step 9: Demonstrate TCP Sockets & Java RMI</h4>
    <p><strong>Action:</strong> Show the terminal running <code>InventoryServer</code> and <code>InventoryClient</code>, and <code>RmiServer</code> and <code>RmiClient</code>.</p>
    <p><strong>What to say to examiner:</strong><br>
    <em>"Finally, here are our distributed networking modules. In shopsphere-network, InventoryServer listens on TCP port 5055 with a thread pool of 10 worker threads. In shopsphere-rmi, RmiServer creates an RMI Registry on port 1099, allowing remote clients to invoke inventory methods across different JVMs."</em></p>
</div>

